1. **Go to Australian Metal Recycling (Formerly Matthews Metal Management) first.** Address: **34 Mountbatten Drive, Dubbo NSW 2830**. Phone: **(02) 6882 1033**. AMR’s own site lists Dubbo at that address and gives **Monday–Friday 7:30am–4:00pm**. It says e-waste is accepted for free, but separately lists lithium batteries as not accepted, which is exactly why you need to speak to the Dubbo yard before relying on it. [AMR Recycling](https://australianmetalrecycling.com.au/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Don’t bring a load on the first visit. Say:

> “I’m setting up a small electronics reuse/refurbishment operation in Dubbo. I’ll have a small amount of non-reusable laptops, PCs, monitors, TVs, phones and parts. I need to understand exactly what this yard will accept from a small commercial operator.”

Then get answers to these exact questions:

- Can I bring **small commercial e-waste loads**, or is the free service household-only?
- Do you accept **laptops with their internal lithium battery still installed**?
- Do phone/tablet batteries need removing?
- Do you accept **CRT TVs and CRT monitors**?
- Do you accept loose PCBs, cables, chargers and dismantled computer parts?
- Is there a minimum quantity?
- Is there a fee for commercial e-waste?
- Can I get a **weighbridge docket / weight ticket**?
- Can I get a **certificate of recycling**?
- Do you provide **secure HDD/SSD destruction**, and if so is it serial-numbered?
- What is the **first physical facility** the Dubbo e-waste goes to after you receive it?
- What company operates that facility?
- Is that facility **AS/NZS 5377 certified**?
- Is the material handled under the **NTCRS**, and if so which co-regulatory arrangement?

Get a name for whoever answers and write the date down.

2. **Then visit Whylandra Waste and Recycling Centre.** Address: **Cooba Road, Dubbo NSW 2830**, about 15 km west of Dubbo, just off Narromine Road/Mitchell Highway. Council lists weekday hours as **8:00am–4:45pm** and says **commercial customers must enter before 4:15pm**. [Dubbo Regional Council](https://www.dubbo.nsw.gov.au/Households-Residents/Rubbish-Recycling-and-Sustainability/whylandra-waste-recycling-centre?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

I could not find a separate public weighbridge phone number, so use **Dubbo Regional Council: (02) 6801 4000** for Whylandra questions. [Dubbo Regional Council](https://www.dubbo.nsw.gov.au/ArticleDocuments/10505/WWRC%20Pre%20Tour%20Information.pdf.aspx?Embed=Y\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

At the weighbridge/office, say:

> “I’m investigating a very small electronics refurbishment business. If I occasionally have non-reusable computers, monitors, TVs and electronics, can I bring that material here as a business rather than as a household resident?”

Ask:

- Is commercial e-waste accepted?
- Is it free under the NTCRS or charged as commercial waste?
- Are there volume limits?
- Laptops with batteries: accepted or battery removed first?
- Phones/tablets?
- CRT televisions?
- Printers?
- Loose computer parts?
- Do they issue a weight docket or recycling receipt?
- Who collects the e-waste from Whylandra now?
- Which downstream facility does it go to?

The goal is to compare **AMR versus Whylandra** as your fallback route.

3. **Go to Dubbo Regional Council and speak to a Duty Planner.** Address: **corner of Church and Darling Streets, Dubbo NSW 2830**. Phone: **(02) 6801 4000**. Council office hours are **Monday–Friday, 9am–5pm**. Duty Planners are available by phone or in person; Council currently says planning advice is available roughly **1pm–4:30pm/5pm Monday–Friday**. [Dubbo Regional Council](https://www.dubbo.nsw.gov.au/i-want-to/contact-council?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Don’t just ask “can I run a business from home?” Give them the exact operation:

> “I want written planning advice on a small electronics reuse/refurbishment operation from an existing shed. Devices would be received by appointment only, stored indoors, tested and repaired, sold mainly online, with non-reusable equipment sent to an external recycler. No shredding or industrial processing. I need to know what land-use definition applies and whether consent is required.”

Ask specifically whether they regard it as:

- a **home business**;
- **goods repair and reuse premises**;
- a **waste or resource management facility**;
- or another defined use.

And ask: **“How do I obtain that answer in writing?”**

Council says formal Written Planning Advice can be requested through DRC&ME after first discussing the matter with a Duty Planner. [Dubbo Regional Council](https://www.dubbo.nsw.gov.au/Builders-Developers/Applying-and-Post-Approval/online-certificates-enquiries-and-advice?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

4. **Call NSW Fair Trading. Do this rather than relying on a counter conversation.** Phone: **13 32 20**, Monday–Friday **8:30am–5pm Sydney time**. [NSW Government](https://www.nsw.gov.au/departments-and-agencies/fair-trading/contact?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Your exact opening question should be:

> “I would receive donated or free laptops, phones and computers, refurbish and resell usable ones, and recycle the failures. Does that require a NSW second-hand dealer licence, or does it fall within the recycling/rubbish collection program exemption?”

Then ask:

- Does receiving something **for free** count as acquiring second-hand goods?
- If licensed, does the **14-day hold** stop you wiping customer data?
- Can you test or repair during the hold?
- Can you erase a drive for privacy while still retaining the prescribed records?
- Can they give you the interpretation **in writing**?

If you want to go somewhere physically, **Dubbo Service Centre**, **258–260 Macquarie Street, Dubbo NSW 2830**, can help with NSW Government services. Service NSW phone: **13 77 88**. The current listing shows Business Bureau staff on weekdays, and they recommend calling to book. [Service NSW](https://www.service.nsw.gov.au/service-centre/dubbo-service-centre?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

5. **Call NSW EPA.** Phone: **131 555**. General enquiry hours are **8:30am–5pm Monday–Friday**. [EPA NSW](https://www.epa.nsw.gov.au/About-us/Contact-us/contacts-services?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Say:

> “I’m proposing a very small electronics reuse/refurbishment operation. I won’t shred or chemically process e-waste. I may dismantle failed computers for reusable parts and send residual material to an external recycler.”

Ask:

- At what point does this become a regulated **waste facility or resource-recovery activity**?
- Does dismantling failed computers count as waste processing at this scale?
- Are there specific requirements for temporarily storing lithium-powered devices?
- What should you do with a damaged/swollen battery discovered after intake?

6. **Get an insurance answer before taking other people’s equipment.** One local starting point is Ausure Insurance Brokers Dubbo, **129–131 Talbragar Street, Dubbo NSW 2830**, phone **(02) 6884 2650**.

Another local option is Elders Insurance Western Plains - Dubbo, **174 Brisbane Street, Dubbo NSW 2830**, phone **(02) 6883 3700**.

Tell the broker you specifically need cover considered for:

- public liability;
- product liability for refurbished electronics;
- customers’ devices/property in your custody;
- lithium-ion battery/fire exposure;
- stock stored at the premises;
- testing and minor repair;
- collection/delivery;
- online resale;
- accidental loss of customer data/privacy incidents.

Ask them to give you the **acceptance/exclusions in writing**, not just “you should be covered.”

